\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent Papers~A--D built a cold-start conversational recommender end to end: a frozen unified set-encoder
(Paper~A), a learned elicitation policy (Paper~B), a continuous off-manifold question policy (Paper~C), and an
open free-recall asker with an interpretable, deployable question tree (Paper~D). All four evaluated against a
\emph{geometric} user simulator. This final paper closes the loop into a real conversation. It makes two
contributions. First, \textbf{interpretability by retrieval, not inversion}: every action of the latent
policies---Paper~C's continuous query $q\in\R^{64}$ and Paper~D's grounded open question---is made human-readable by
\emph{snapping} to a fixed phrase bank and rendering with an LLM, and we report the \emph{snap-loss} as the honest cost
of naming. Second, \textbf{a deployed conversational agent}: an LLM renders each grounded question as natural language
and parses the user's free-text answer back to a catalogue entity (name $\to$ nearest factor), and a chat interface runs
the Paper~D question tree live, folding answers into the belief and \emph{rendering recommendations} (titles and posters)
that update every turn. We close with a small human round-trip study that replaces the geometric answerer band with real
recall, and a head-to-head against a genuine LLM-in-the-loop elicitor.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction \& positioning}
\label{sec:intro}
\todo{One paragraph recap of A--D: frozen unified encoder; the elicitation problem; open free-recall beats latent
rating-probes; the deployable adaptive asker + interpretable tree. The tension this paper resolves: every result so far
was measured against a \emph{geometric simulator}, and the policies act in a $64$-dimensional latent space. To be a
product, the agent must (i) speak natural language at both ends, and (ii) be interpretable. This paper does both and
ships a working interface.}

\section{Interpretability by retrieval, not inversion}
\label{sec:interp}
\paragraph{Why not embedding inversion.} Vec2Text/GEIA \emph{decode} embeddings to text but are per-encoder, lossy, and
unreliable on the sentence encoders we use; we never invert. Interpretation is \emph{retrieval}: snap to a fixed phrase
bank.
\paragraph{The phrase bank.} \todo{Mine short descriptive phrases (``slow-burn revenge'', ``feel-good ensemble'') from
movie reviews / plot keywords / genome glosses; place each in the recommender's $\R^{64}$ factor space as the centroid
of its matched movies (exactly how genome concepts were built). For Paper~D the ``phrases'' are already named
(favourite/hidden-gem/disliked movie, actor, director, genre)---interpretability is native; the snapping is only needed
for Paper~C's off-manifold continuous queries.}
\paragraph{Snap-loss (the honesty test).} \todo{Report NDCG of the un-snapped continuous query vs.\ the
snapped-to-bank query (Paper~C's snap-loss): the gap is the cost of naming. Paper~D's open questions are already named,
so their snap-loss is zero---a deployability advantage of recall over latent probing.}
\paragraph{The learned question tree as the interpretable backbone.} \todo{Reproduce Paper~D's tree (fig) as the agent's
visible decision logic: open with ``what do you like?'', branch on the answer into person / all-time-favourite /
hidden-gem / disliked, deepen into a continuous probe when recall saturates.}

\section{The conversational agent}
\label{sec:agent}
\paragraph{Render: question $\to$ natural language.} \todo{An LLM renders each grounded question type as a natural
utterance (``what's a film you love that you think is underrated?'') conditioned on the running belief; templated
fallback for offline use.}
\paragraph{Parse: free text $\to$ entity.} \todo{The user answers in free text (``Pan's Labyrinth''); we ground it to a
catalogue entity by fuzzy title / SBERT match to the nearest factor $Q_j$, fold $(Q_j,\,\mathrm{POS})$ into the belief,
re-rank. Persons/genres ground to their film-centroids (Paper~D). This closes the geometric$\leftrightarrow$language loop
the simulator stood in for.}
\paragraph{Render recommendations.} \todo{Every turn, show the top-$K$ recommendations (titles $+$ TMDB posters) that
update as the belief sharpens---the user watches the list improve as they answer.}

